Here's the full diff of the chat template change I made, shown here as a diff against the chat template shipped with the larger Gemma 4 (26B and 31B) models (can be adapted to the smaller ones as well, but the base templates are slightly different):

```diff
--- chat_template_gemma_large_orig.jinja	2026-04-16 19:13:08.903438297 -0400
+++ chat_template_gemma_large_fixed.jinja	2026-04-16 20:21:41.983833992 -0400
@@ -186,7 +186,15 @@
     {%- endif -%}

     {%- if messages[0]['role'] in ['system', 'developer'] -%}
-        {{- messages[0]['content'] | trim -}}
+        {%- if messages[0]['content'] is string -%}
+            {{- messages[0]['content'] | trim -}}
+        {%- elif messages[0]['content'] is sequence -%}
+            {%- for item in messages[0]['content'] -%}
+                {%- if item['type'] == 'text' -%}
+                    {{- item['text'] | trim -}}
+                {%- endif -%}
+            {%- endfor -%}
+        {%- endif -%}
         {%- set loop_messages = messages[1:] -%}
     {%- endif -%}

@@ -302,19 +310,34 @@
                 {%- endfor -%}
             {%- endif -%}

+            {%- set ns_text = namespace(ok=false) -%}
             {%- if message['content'] is string -%}
                 {%- if role == 'model' -%}
-                    {{- strip_thinking(message['content']) -}}
+                    {%- set _s = strip_thinking(message['content']) -%}
+                    {{- _s -}}
+                    {%- if (_s | trim | length) > 0 -%}
+                        {%- set ns_text.ok = true -%}
+                    {%- endif -%}
                 {%- else -%}
                     {{- message['content'] | trim -}}
+                    {%- if (message['content'] | trim | length) > 0 -%}
+                        {%- set ns_text.ok = true -%}
+                    {%- endif -%}
                 {%- endif -%}
             {%- elif message['content'] is sequence -%}
                 {%- for item in message['content'] -%}
                     {%- if item['type'] == 'text' -%}
                         {%- if role == 'model' -%}
-                            {{- strip_thinking(item['text']) -}}
+                            {%- set _s = strip_thinking(item['text']) -%}
+                            {{- _s -}}
+                            {%- if (_s | trim | length) > 0 -%}
+                                {%- set ns_text.ok = true -%}
+                            {%- endif -%}
                         {%- else -%}
                             {{- item['text'] | trim -}}
+                            {%- if (item['text'] | trim | length) > 0 -%}
+                                {%- set ns_text.ok = true -%}
+                            {%- endif -%}
                         {%- endif -%}
                     {%- elif item['type'] == 'image' -%}
                         {{- '<|image|>' -}}
@@ -331,7 +354,7 @@

         {%- if ns.prev_message_type == 'tool_call' and not ns_tr_out.flag -%}
             {{- '<|tool_response>' -}}
-        {%- elif not (ns_tr_out.flag and not message.get('content')) -%}
+        {%- elif not (ns_tr_out.flag and not ns_text.ok) -%}
             {{- '<turn|>\n' -}}
         {%- endif -%}
     {%- endif -%}
```

The first change in that diff is to handle how OpenCode sends in the system role not as a string (which the template expected) but as a text content part. This was resulting in the entire system prompt from OpenCode getting rendered to the model as a JSON array instead instead of as a simple text prompt.

The second change in that diff is because OpenCode is not sending in the exact same messages it received, and instead including an empty text content part for the `content` of every tool call message instead of setti

I think that the proper way to implement this is to allow adding configuration that specifies one or more regular expressions that the UI highlights in printed text, and if clicked, generates an event with the matched expression as parameter that can be caught in a plugin.

For example,
```
  "[/A-Za-z0-9_-]+\.ts:[0-9]+": "typescript_line"
```
and in a plugin:
```
export const HighlightPlugin = async (input) => {
  return {
    "clicked.env": async (ctx) {
      ..do something with ctx.key, that will be equal to "typescript_line"..
      ..and ctx.link, that will be equal to say "packages/opencode/src/session/llm.ts:149"
    }
  };
};
```

This seems to be an issue where the metadata on models.dev isn't setup correctly. This can be overwritten for now with a custom model config until they push the correction.

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "opencode-go": {
      "models": {
        "qwen3.6-plus": {
          "limit": {
            "context": 1000000,
            "output": 65536
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Quick Guide: Using this PR locally (without models.dev)

Since the [models.dev PR](https://github.com/anomalyco/models.dev/pull/1312) hasn't been merged yet, here's how to get Kiro working with just this PR — no need to build models.dev separately.

### 1. Clone & checkout

```bash
git clone https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode.git
cd opencode
gh pr checkout 20491
bun install
```

### 2. Configure models manually

Since Kiro isn't on models.dev yet, add models to your **global** config at `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` (Linux/macOS):

```jsonc
{
  "provider": {
    "kiro": {
      "npm": "kiro-ai-provider",
      "name": "Kiro",
      "models": {
        "claude-opus-4.7": {
          "name": "Claude Opus 4.7",
          "attachment": true, "reasoning": true, "tool_call": true, "temperature": true,
          "limit": { "context": 1000000, "output": 64000 }
        },
        "claude-opus-4.6": {
          "name": "Claude Opus 4.6",
          "attachment": true, "reasoning": true, "tool_call": true, "temperature": true,
          "limit": { "context": 1000000, "output": 64000 }
        },
        "claude-sonnet-4.6": {
          "name": "Claude Sonnet 4.6",
          "attachment": true, "reasoning": true, "tool_call": true, "temperature": true,
          "limit": { "context": 1000000, "output": 64000 }
        },
        "auto": {
          "name": "Kiro Auto",
          "attachment": true, "reasoning": true, "tool_call": true, "temperature": true,
          "limit": { "context": 1000000, "output": 64000 }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

> You can list all available model IDs by running:
> ```bash
> cd opencode/packages/opencode && bun -e "import { listModels } from 'kiro-ai-provider'; listModels().then(m => console.log(m.map(x => x.modelId)))"
> ```

### 3. Authenticate

**Option A — API Key** (simplest):

Create `~/.aws/sso/cache/kiro-auth-token.json`:
```json
{
  "accessToken": "ksk-YOUR_API_KEY_HERE",
  "refreshToken": "",
  "expiresAt": "2027-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
  "region": "us-east-1"
}
```

**Option B — OAuth** (Builder ID / IAM Identity Center):
```bash
bun dev providers login --provider kiro
```

### 4. Run

```bash
bun dev                    # runs in packages/opencode dir
bun dev ~/your-project     # runs in a specific directory
```

Use **Ctrl+M** to switch to a Kiro model.

### Note

- `claude-opus-4.7` was just added to Kiro but may not be rolled out to all regions yet. If it errors, use `claude-opus-4.6` instead.
- For a standalone binary: `./packages/opencode/script/build.ts --single`, output at `./packages/opencode/dist/opencode-<platform>/bin/opencode`.
